===Motivation===

Liquid wallets ([https://github.com/Blockstream/lwk LWK], Elements Core, Jade) already implement CT descriptors, blinded PSETs, and Esplora/Electrum sync. None of them offer a published receive identifier that stays unlinkable across payments.

Merchants, donation pages, and standing counterparty relationships currently choose between:

* reusing a confidential <code>lq1...</code> address — CT hides amounts, but every payment shares the same script; or
* distributing fresh addresses interactively — unlinkable scripts, but operationally costly.

<code>spv0</code> removes that trade-off. A receiver publishes one <code>lsp1...</code> address; each sender derives a unique confidential P2WPKH output. Third parties see only unrelated one-time scripts and blinded commitments; the receiver detects payments by scan and recovers values with a blinding key tied to the same ECDH secret that derived the spend key.

Wallet libraries need one normative specification for the cross-protocol seams — address HRP, blinding derivation, scan loops, indexer API, spend-key handling — so implementations interoperate without fork-specific behaviour.

==Scope and dependencies==

===In scope===

* <code>spv0</code> on Liquid mainnet, testnet, and regtest.
* Address encoding, BIP-32 paths, <code>elsp</code> descriptor.
* Send path (PSET); receive paths (full node, tweak server).
* Software signing with per-UTXO tweaked keys.
* Minimal Liquid tweak-server HTTP API.

===Out of scope===

* Consensus, opcode, or relay-policy changes.
* Confidential Taproot (<code>eltr</code>) silent-payment outputs — reserved for <code>spv1</code>.
* Hardware-signer firmware; CoinJoin / multi-party input aggregation without DLEQ proofs.
* Remote-scanner and personalized-scanner stacks from the index-server specification.

